[inference]
template_type: KnowAct
contract:
  input:
    target_signal: string
    telemetry_events: array
    filtered_count: integer
    replayed_count: integer
    existing_regressions: array
    userpod_host: string
  output:
    signal_sources: array
    regressions_checked: array
    signal: object
visibility: Public
---
{# goal: Select signal sources from the recorded reg_query telemetry (step 1). #}
{# The reg_query return shape: {namespace, window_seconds, replayed_count, #}
{#   filtered_count, events: [{timestamp, namespace, path, phase, weight, #}
{#   observation}]}. filtered_count is the real signal volume (replay_weighted #}
{#   applies the SQL limit BEFORE the namespace filter). #}
{# P5: minimal — no synthetic signals; only observe spans actually emitted #}
{#   by the running system and recorded by reg_query. #}
{# P9: emit reg.runtime.select span with discovered signal sources and #}
{#   regression count #}
{# P12: every selection action carries userpod/owner identity #}

Select signal: {{ target_signal | default('all') }}
Telemetry events (from reg_query, step 1): {{ telemetry_events | default([]) }}
Filtered count (real signal volume): {{ filtered_count | default(0) }}
Replayed count (pre-filter, pre-weight): {{ replayed_count | default(0) }}
Existing runtime regressions: {{ existing_regressions | default([]) }}
Defense layers to verify (runtime firing): regulation_loop, action_distribution_monitoring

NOTE: reg_query's replay_weighted applies the SQL limit BEFORE the namespace
filter, so replayed_count may exceed filtered_count. filtered_count is the
real signal volume for the `reg.` namespace within the observation window.
If filtered_count is 0, the running system emitted no reg.* spans in the
window — return empty signal_sources and recommend deploying a regulation-
aware component. Do NOT fabricate signals.

ACTION: Emit `reg.runtime.select` span (P9, P12) with discovered signal sources,
selected signal type, and regression count.
ACTION: Read `security/regressions/` for entries with `surface: runtime` and
`status: enforced`.
NOTE: If no `surface: runtime` entries exist yet, return empty list — this
skill bootstraps the runtime surface.
ACTION: Return JSON: `{signal, signal_sources: [...], signal_types: [...],
defense_layers: [...], existing_regressions: [...], userpod_host}`.
CONSTRAINT: Do NOT fabricate signal sources. Only list span targets that the
running system actually emitted and reg_query recorded.
CONSTRAINT: Each discovered signal source must include concrete span target
string (e.g., `reg.outcome.action_blocked`, `reg.tool.invoked`) drawn from
the telemetry_events.
CONSTRAINT: Use `public` visibility; no hidden runtime checks (P3, P4).
CONSTRAINT: Registry (this manifest.yaml + templates) is authoritative over
SKILL.md (P5.1).
CONSTRAINT: This skill is complementary to `supply-chain-sentinel` (static
manifest audit, P4 manifest boundary) — this skill observes runtime behavior
(P4 runtime boundary). Zero overlap.
CONSTRAINT: This skill is complementary to `kali-audit` (static defense-layer
presence check) — this skill observes whether those defense layers actually
fire at runtime. Zero overlap.
CONSTRAINT: This skill is complementary to `adversarial-red-team` (synthetic
LLM I/O attacks) — this skill observes real runtime traffic. Zero overlap.
CONSTRAINT: Reference concrete taxonomy: MITRE CWE-1357 (Reliance on Component
Not Updateable — runtime dependency behavior), CWE-829 (Inclusion from
Untrusted Control Sphere — runtime untrusted input), CWE-200 (Information
Exposure — runtime endpoint exposure); OWASP LLM06 (Excessive Agency —
runtime tool misuse), LLM07 (System Prompt Leakage — runtime canary
detection); MITRE ATLAS AML.TA0010 (Exfiltration — runtime data exfiltration).
CONSTRAINT: Every runtime threat finding must cite span target, timestamp,
signal value, and evidence snippet — not summary description alone.
CONSTRAINT: If target_signal is 'all', discover all `reg.*` canonical spans
emitted by the running system and recorded by reg_query. Prioritize
`reg.outcome.*` and `reg.tool.*` since they carry security-relevant runtime
signals.
CONSTRAINT: Read actual runtime telemetry (from reg_query, not synthetic)
before proposing any regression entry.
CONSTRAINT: Every finding must include `userpod_host` identity (P12) — no
anonymous runtime scanning.
CONSTRAINT: Apply pragmatic-cybernetics (like `bug-hunt` probe phase): trace
signal polarity (does the signal indicate increasing or decreasing risk?),
check variety (are there alternative signal sources for the same threat?),
perform Good Regulator check (is the defense layer actually regulating the
threat?).
CONSTRAINT: If `security/regressions/` contains `surface: runtime` entries,
read them as input and skip proposing duplicates.
CONSTRAINT: Propose `surface: runtime` regression format: `id: RR-XXXX`,
`surface: runtime`, `cwe: CWE-XXX`, `owasp_llm: LLMXX`, `atlas_tactic:
AML.TAXXXX`, `discovered_in: <span_target>`, `status: pending` (human must
flip to `enforced`).
CONSTRAINT: Do NOT invent CVE numbers or vulnerability descriptions. Only
propose regressions for patterns actually discovered in runtime telemetry:
regulation plateaus, tool invocation anomalies, inference rate spikes.
CONSTRAINT: If signal discovery finds zero runtime signals (filtered_count ==
0), return empty signal_sources and recommend `signal: regulation` based on
deployed components — do not invent signals.
CONSTRAINT: The 5W1H gate applies: Who (running application / userpod host),
What (runtime signal / span target), When (observation window / timestamp),
Where (deployed runtime environment / production workload), Why (P3.1 safe
container requires runtime blocking / P1 sovereignty over runtime behavior /
P4 clear boundary — runtime behavior must be explicitly observed), How
(discover + observe + classify + regulate + report + propose regression).
CONSTRAINT: When referencing security taxonomy: use MITRE CWE (not invented),
OWASP LLM 2025 (existing categories), and MITRE ATLAS (existing tactics) —
not generic descriptions.
CONSTRAINT: Minimal — 4 templates only, no bundle, no sub-agent delegation, no
extra abstraction layer. Each template answers a specific 5W1H question
(select: Where; classify: What + How; regulate: Why + What; convergence-check:
When + Why).
CONSTRAINT: Every output must include evidence-backed span references (from
reg_query, not synthetic) and source citations (hkask-regulation docs, MITRE
CWE list, OWASP LLM 2025 reference, MITRE ATLAS reference).
CONSTRAINT: Do NOT claim runtime coverage that hasn't been verified through
actual signal observation (reg_query). Convergence metric must reflect real
runtime coverage, not aspirational.
CONSTRAINT: Each runtime threat finding proposal must use the exact YAML format
from `security/regressions/README.md`: `id`, `title`, `surface: runtime`, `cwe`,
`owasp_llm_2025`, `atlas_tactic`, `discovered_in`, `discovered_by`,
`discovered_at`, `severity`, `detection`, `mitigation`, `ci_gate`, `status`.
CONSTRAINT: This skill is NOT an EDR/MDR replacement for Huntress; it is a
hKask-native runtime posture skill that observes Regulation telemetry, applies
security taxonomy, proposes CI-enforced regressions, and tracks defense-layer
firing coverage. It operates within user sovereignty (P1) — reads only local
Regulation telemetry, makes no external network calls unless user grants
consent (P2), produces transparent regression proposals (P3), respects
runtime boundaries (P4), minimizes to essential checks (P5), provides
generative space for userpod agents (P6), evolves from real runtime evidence
(P7), grounds findings in semantic taxonomy (P8), regulates through
Regulation spans (P9), operates under explicit bot/userpod taxonomy (P10),
respects shared/public visibility (P11), requires host identity (P12).
CONSTRAINT: This skill does NOT download external packages or scan OS-level
endpoints. It observes hKask's own Regulation telemetry only (P4 boundary —
runtime Regulation boundary, not OS endpoint boundary).
